{title: Be Thou My Vision}
{lyricist: Eleanor H. Hull}
{translator: Mary E. Byrne}
{composer: Irish folk melody}
{year: 1912}
{meta: translation-year 1905}
{key: D}
{time: 3/4}
{meta: tune SLANE}
{meta: meter 10.10.9.10}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to SLANE}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: note From an Irish hymn of about the 8th century. Byrne translated it into English prose and Hull put it into verse.}
{meta: tags devotion, guidance}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
Be [D]Thou my [G]Vision, O [D]Lord of my [A]heart;
[D]Naught be all [G]else to me, [Bm]save that Thou [A]art;
[Bm]Thou my best [G]Thought, by [D]day or by [Bm]night,
[G]Waking or [D]sleeping, Thy [A]presence my [D]light.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Be [D]Thou my [G]Wisdom, and [D]Thou my true [A]Word;
[D]I ever [G]with Thee and [Bm]Thou with me, [A]Lord;
[Bm]Thou my great [G]Father, [D]I Thy true [Bm]son;
[G]Thou in me [D]dwelling, and [A]I with Thee [D]one.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[D]Riches I [G]heed not, nor [D]man's empty [A]praise,
[D]Thou mine In[G]heritance, [Bm]now and al[A]ways:
[Bm]Thou and Thou [G]only, [D]first in my [Bm]heart,
[G]High King of [D]Heaven, my [A]Treasure Thou [D]art.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[D]High King of [G]Heaven, my [D]victory [A]won,
[D]May I reach [G]Heaven's joys, O [Bm]bright Heaven's [A]Sun!
[Bm]Heart of my [G]own heart, what[D]ever be[Bm]fall,
[G]Still be my [D]Vision, O [A]Ruler of [D]all.
{end_of_verse}
